\paragraph{Epoch confound on Few-NERD.}
Few-NERD positive results should be interpreted cautiously: the 3-epoch model may be under-converged, and 5-epoch training eliminates LP gains entirely ($+1.39{\to}+0.09{\pm}0.04$\,pp, 94\% collapse; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
Entity construction likewise degrades: LP-weighted $\Delta$ drops from $+0.88$ to $+0.20$\,pp (seed 42, macro-F1; $77$\% reduction).
The diagnostic framework correctly predicts this: 5-epoch constant-F1 degeneracy rises to $31$\% (vs.\ $13$\% at 3 epochs; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}), degrading the conditions for both selection and construction.\footnote{Degeneracy rates vary by definition: constant-F1 degeneracy (all $N$ samples yield identical F1) gives $31$\%/$13$\%; entity-set exact-match degeneracy gives $26$\%/$9$\%. The directional pattern is consistent.}
This sensitivity---favorable conditions are fragile and training-dependent---reinforces that Few-NERD is a conditional success rather than evidence of a general bypass. The core negative finding (selection ${\leq}$ greedy under high degeneracy) is unaffected.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Training convergence modulates scaling returns.}
Training convergence state is itself a determinant of SC efficacy: 5-epoch training on Few-NERD eliminates LP gains entirely (LP selection: $+1.39{\to}+0.09{\pm}0.04$\,pp, 94\% reduction; entity construction: $+0.88{\to}+0.20$\,pp, 77\% reduction; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
The diagnostic framework is consistent with this observation: 5-epoch constant-F1 degeneracy rises to $31$\% (vs.\ $13$\% at 3 epochs), degrading conditions for both selection and construction.\footnote{Degeneracy rates vary by definition: constant-F1 degeneracy (all $N$ samples yield identical F1) gives $31$\%/$13$\%; entity-set exact-match degeneracy gives $26$\%/$9$\%. The directional pattern is consistent.}
This suggests SC may complement early stopping strategies---under-converged models preserve output diversity that enables aggregation gains, while fully converged models exhibit degeneracy that renders scaling ineffective.

\subsection{Trained Verifiers: High Discrimination, Low Transfer}\label{sec:verifier}

A LightGBM entity-level classifier (5 features: agreement count, LP, VC, SJ, FK) achieves AUC${=}0.923{\pm}0.002$ out-of-fold but translates to only $+0.30$\,pp overall: frequency-based construction already captures most verifier signal.
Cross-dataset transfer is negative ($-0.76$ to $-1.02$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:verifier_transfer}), as feature importance shifts across entity-type complexity---even near-perfect entity classification cannot close the gap.

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Concurrent work.}
\citet{landesberg2026bon} independently document a correlation-selection gap in best-of-$N$ reasoning: global judge $r{=}0.47$ masks within-prompt $r{=}0.27$ (Table~\ref{tab:novelty_comparison}).
This concurrent discovery validates the gap as a cross-domain phenomenon; our contribution is the mechanistic diagnosis specific to structured prediction---entity-set degeneracy and LP compression have no analogue in single-score reasoning.

